    "title": "Start a site with <span class=\"chulapa\">Chulapa</span>",
    "subtitle": "Publish your first site, then make it your own",
    "excerpt": "Create a blog, edit your first post and add a photograph using GitHub in your browser.",
    "content": "Start with the GitHub template You only need a free GitHub account and your browser. The Chulapa 101 template includes a blog, sample articles, an About page and search. Preview it, then follow these steps. 1. Create your site Sign in to GitHub and open the template. Choose a Repository name, for example my-blog, and create a public repository. A repository is the folder containing your site’s files on GitHub. In your new repository, open Settings &gt; Pages. Under Build and deployment, choose GitHub Actions as the source. 2. Change the name and author In Code, open _config.yml and click the pencil button. Replace these existing values with your own details; do not add duplicate entries. For GitHub user ana and repository my-blog: title: \"Ana's blog\" description: \"Stories and photographs from my travels\" repository: ana/my-blog url: \"https://ana.github.io\" Use your own username and repository name. Find the existing author section and change name to your name. Keep the indentation of the other entries: author: name: \"Ana\" Click Commit changes to save to the default branch (main or master). This starts publishing automatically. Leave the other settings, including baseurl, unchanged for now; the template sets the website path for you. 3. Open your published website In Actions, wait for the latest publishing run to succeed. Open the website address in Settings &gt; Pages: your blog should show its new name. If publishing fails, open the failed run and check its error; see troubleshooting. 4. Write your first post In Code, open a sample article in _posts/ and click the pencil button. Change title in the settings between the --- lines, keeping the other settings. Replace the text below that block with your story: This is my first story. ## A place I visited I enjoyed **walking by the river**. [Read more about the place](https://example.org/) Save with Commit changes. After publishing finishes, refresh your website and open the edited article. For a new article rather than an edited sample, see Create a post. Also edit _pages/about.md to introduce yourself and replace the sample contact details. Keep the home page filename index.html. 5. Add a photograph In your repository, open assets/img/, choose Add file &gt; Upload files, and upload a photograph named, for example, river.jpg. Save the upload. Edit your post and add the following where you want the image to appear. Change the description to describe your photograph: ![A riverside path beneath green trees]({{ '/assets/img/river.jpg' | relative_url }}) Save the post, wait for publishing and check that the photograph appears. Keep the relative_url part so the image address works on your site. Describe the photograph for readers who cannot see it, and add a credit if required. 6. Choose a design You can keep the current design or choose one from the skin previews. A skin is a ready-made combination of colors and fonts. In _config.yml, find chulapa-skin and change its existing skin value: chulapa-skin: skin: journal Keep any other entries in that section. Save, wait for publishing and refresh your website to...",
