    "title": "Theming <span class=\"chulapa\">Chulapa</span>",
    "subtitle": "Customize colors, fonts and components",
    "excerpt": "Chulapa lets you customize colors, fonts, spacing, borders and buttons. Use predefined skins or set individual variables to give each site its own appearance.",
    "content": "Chulapa lets you customize colors, fonts, spacing, borders and buttons. Use predefined skins or set individual variables to give each site its own appearance. Set built-in theme variables in _config.yml. Use custom CSS for changes beyond those variables. Restart Jekyll after editing _config.yml. Theme variables are compiled into CSS during the build. Skins A skin is a ready-made design with coordinated colors and fonts. Choose one from the skin previews, then open _config.yml and change the existing chulapa-skin.skin entry: chulapa-skin: skin: journal Keep any other entries under chulapa-skin. Save your changes, wait for publishing and refresh the site. Try gitdev, lux or flatly for other looks. Use the exact lowercase name shown by the preview: an unknown name prevents the site from building. Change one color To adjust a skin rather than replace it, add or edit vars in the same chulapa-skin section. This example chooses a skin and changes its main color: chulapa-skin: skin: journal vars: primary: \"#285d70\" Keep the quotes around colors beginning with #. Check text and link contrast after publishing; a color change can affect more than one part of the page. See the variable dictionary for menu and footer colors. Load Google Fonts This is optional: each skin already includes a font choice. To use a different font, choose it at Google Fonts and add its stylesheet URL under googlefonts in _config.yml. Then apply it to text and headings: googlefonts: - url: \"https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Raleway&amp;family=Rubik&amp;display=swap\" chulapa-skin: skin: journal vars: font-family-base: \"Rubik, sans-serif\" headings-font-family: \"Raleway, sans-serif\" Merge these entries with your existing settings rather than creating a second chulapa-skin section. After publishing, your text should use Rubik and your headings Raleway. For another provider, add its font stylesheet to _includes/custom/custom_head.html following the provider’s instructions. Then set font-family-base and/or headings-font-family as in the example above: loading a font alone does not apply it. See custom HTML and styles for other ways to add fonts. Code highlighting Choose how code blocks look from the style previews. In _config.yml, change or add highlight under your existing chulapa-skin section, keeping your other settings: chulapa-skin: highlight: zenburn The default style is default. Save, publish and check a page containing a code block to see the change. Autothemer Optional, for more control over colors. You can keep the skin unchanged if you do not need a generated palette. Autothemer derives a color palette from primary, including colors for warnings and errors. Explicit colors from the skin or chulapa-skin.vars take precedence over its defaults. You can combine autothemer with a skin. Its SCSS variables use !default, so values already defined by the skin or vars remain in effect. A skin can limit the visible effect of autothemer. Use vars to change colors the skin already defines. Autothemer fills undefined values; it does not replace an existing palette. To enable autothemer: chulapa-skin: autothemer : true #omit to disable this option Variables Advanced reference. Sass is the styling language used by the theme. You can set its supported variables through YAML without editing the theme itself. Chulapa...",
