    "title": "Accessibility and performance",
    "subtitle": "Check your site's navigation, colors and resources",
    "excerpt": "Keyboard behavior, reduced motion and practical loading advice.",
    "content": "Make sure visitors can read and use your site on a phone, with a keyboard and with enlarged text. Start with your own content and the checks below. Check your own pages Use Tab to move through links and controls. Focus should remain visible; Enter opens links, and menu buttons also work with Space. Check the home page, an article and search on a narrow screen. Enlarge text and confirm that content stays readable and controls remain reachable. Describe informative photographs and use meaningful link text, such as “View my portfolio”, instead of “Click here”. Start article sections with ##, below the page title. Check text and link contrast after changing a skin or colors. Try search, comments and any widgets you have enabled. The improvements described here are development changes after v2.1.0, not part of the v2.1.0 gem or pinned remote theme. Your content, colors and integrations still need checking; the theme alone does not establish compliance with the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines (WCAG). Keyboard navigation The first Tab stop, Skip to content, bypasses the menu. Open the floating menu with Enter or Space; Escape closes it and returns focus to its button. Following a link also closes it. Open the sidebar table of contents, then Tab through its close button and links. Escape closes the panel and returns focus. The table of contents highlights the section you are reading without moving keyboard focus. In PhotoSwipe galleries, use arrow keys to browse and Escape to close. Focus returns to the image link. If the viewer cannot load, links still open the original images. See PhotoSwipe lightboxes. With JavaScript disabled, the floating menu provides ordinary navigation links. Interactive galleries and other integrations may need JavaScript and an internet connection. Colors and content Keep links distinguishable from ordinary text. The theme underlines article links and provides visible keyboard focus; check that your custom colors preserve those cues. Carousels have a play/pause control when automatic cycling is enabled. A plain symbol remains if Font Awesome cannot load. They start paused for visitors who prefer reduced motion and stop when someone interacts with the slides or navigation controls. Hover pauses temporarily; use the play control to resume a manually paused slideshow. See carousel settings. Give videos a descriptive name and supply captions or transcripts where needed. For Mermaid diagrams, use accTitle and accDescr. Gallery snippets use generic image descriptions; use custom image markup with descriptive alt text when the photograph conveys information. Check long titles and enlarged text on your actual pages. If you add custom layouts or widgets, also check their headings, labels and keyboard controls. PWA and offline behavior The theme does not provide offline support or a setting to enable it. A favicon or web app manifest alone does not make the site work offline or guarantee that visitors can install it as an app. Adding those capabilities requires a separate setup for your own site and hosting. Comments, embedded videos and the documentation chatbot need an internet connection. Test...",
